'''Gajirrawoong''', also written '''Gajirrabeng''', '''Gadjerawang''',  '''Gadjerong''', '''Gadyerong''' and '''Kajirrawung''', is an [[Australian Aboriginal languages|Australian Aboriginal language]] of the [[Kimberley (Western Australia)|Kimberley]] region in [[Western Australia]]. It is at least [[endangered language|endangered]] and possibly [[extinct language|extinct]]; as of 2004 it was known by only three or four fluent speakers,<ref>{{cite book |last=McGregor |first=William |year=2004 |title=The Languages of the Kimberley, Western Australia |location=London, New York |publisher=Taylor & Francis}}</ref> and in the [[2016 Australian Census]], there were no recorded speakers using it at home.<ref>{{cite web | title=Census 2016, Language spoken at home by Sex (SA2+) | website=[[Australian Bureau of Statistics]] | url=http://stat.data.abs.gov.au/Index.aspx?DataSetCode=ABS_C16_T09_SA | access-date=9 November 2020 | archive-date=26 December 2018 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181226044803/http://stat.data.abs.gov.au/Index.aspx?DataSetCode=ABS_C16_T09_SA | url-status=dead }}</ref>  It is in the [[Jarrakan languages|Jarrakan language family]],<ref name=AIATSIS/> and is the language of the [[Gajirrawoong]] people.

The nearby [[Gurindji language]] is known to have borrowed from Gajirrawoong.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://wold.clld.org/language/130|title=Language: Gajirrabeng|work=World Loanword Database|publisher=Max Planck Society|access-date=10 February 2020}}</ref>

